            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "{\"result\":\"{\\\"success\\\": true, \\\"data\\\": {\\\"stdout\\\": \\\"/root/gated_mfrm_judge_bias/paper/introduction.tex:6:Existing methods address these distortions in isolation. Judge bias corrections---CyclicJudge \\\\\\\\citep{cyclicjudge2026}, UDA \\\\\\\\citep{uda2026}---remove systematic inter-judge differences without modeling contamination. Contamination detection tools---ConStat \\\\\\\\citep{constat2024}, DCR \\\\\\\\citep{dcr2025}, LogProber \\\\\\\\citep{logprober2024}---adjust for memorization without accounting for judge effects (see Section~\\\\\\\\ref{sec:related_work} for detailed review). A bias-first pipeline absorbs contamination-induced score inflation into ability estimates, because the bias model attributes all residual systematic variation to genuine ability. A contamination-first pipeline misattributes judge severity differences as contamination, because score elevation from a lenient judge is indistinguishable from elevation due to memorization without a joint model. In either ordering, the first correction stage produces estimates distorted by the unmodeled facet, and the second stage inherits this distortion.\\\\n/root/gated_mfrm_judge_bias/paper/limitations.tex:7:Code and data will be released upon acceptance at [anonymized URL].\\\\n/root/gated_mfrm_judge_bias/paper/main.tex:3:\\\\\\\\usepackage[review]{acl}\\\\n/root/gated_mfrm_judge_bias/paper/main.tex:27:\\\\\\\\author{Anonymous}\\\\n\\\", \\\"stderr\\\": \\\"\\\", \\\"return_code\\\": 0}, \\\"error\\\": null, \\\"metadata\\\": {\\\"elapsed_seconds\\\": 0.45}}\"}"
            }
